  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAlthough an isolated shower could pop up along the northeast fringe \nof the forecast area later this afternoon, high pressure will \nsupport mostly quiet weather across the area through Tuesday. The \nnext chance of showers, and perhaps thunderstorms, arrives as a \nshortwave steers a front into the area late Tuesday night into \nWednesday. \n\nThe forecast becomes less certain during the day Wednesday as some \nmodels introduce drier air for the afternoon and evening, while \nothers maintain a chance of precipitation in the vicinity of a \nwarm front. Some stronger storms could be possible if activity \nwere to develop later in the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA low pressure system traverses the Great Lakes Region Wednesday \nnight into Thursday and then continues northeast into Quebec by the \nend of the work week. Rain and thunderstorms are expected to spread \ninto the area ahead of a cold front late Wednesday night into \nThursday, then activity continues as the front crosses the area \nThursday and Thursday night. \n\nWhile there will be potential for strong to severe thunderstorms in \nconjunction with the frontal passage, the severe weather risk for \nThursday could be somewhat tempered by clouds and/or activity that \noccurs earlier in the day. Moisture feeding into the area from a \nGulf system could also heighten the risk of excessive rainfall \nacross the area Thursday into Friday. \n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the front, allowing for a quieter \nfinish to the week. Another system then arrives early next week.",
